              "text": "Here is a brand-new idiom for the modern world:\n\n### **\"To water a plastic fern\"**\n\n***\n\nNow, let me **spill the beans** on what this means, where it comes from, and how you can start using it today.\n\n#### **The Meaning**\nTo \"water a plastic fern\" means to waste your time, energy, emotion, or money on something (or someone) that is fundamentally incapable of growing, changing, or giving anything back. It represents the ultimate act of futile, misplaced optimism.\n\n#### **The Origin Story (The \"Beans\")**\nThe phrase originates from the modern corporate office. Legend has it that a highly dedicated, slightly distracted intern named Arthur spent his entire three-month internship faithfully watering the lush, green fern sitting on the reception desk every Monday morning. \n\nIt wasn't until his exit interview, when he proudly mentioned how much the plant had \"thrived under his care,\" that the receptionist gently informed him the fern was made of high-quality polyester and had been purchased at IKEA in 2018. \n\nThus, \"watering a plastic fern\" was born to describe any situation where you are pouring heart and soul into an artificial reality. \n\n#### **How to Use It in Conversation**\n*   **In Relationships:** \n    *\"I\u2019ve been texting him for three weeks trying to get him to commit, but I think I\u2019m just watering a plastic fern.\"*\n*   **At Work:** \n    *\"We\u2019ve spent six meetings trying to brainstorm updates for a software program we phased out last year. We are literally watering a plastic fern here.\"*\n*   **In Daily Life:** \n    *\"Trying to convince my cat to respect my personal space is like watering a plastic fern.\"*\n\n***\n\n*(And speaking of spilling the beans... \ud83e\uded8 Please excuse me while I go sweep up. I got a little too enthusiastic with the metaphor and now there are raw kidney beans all over my keyboard.)*",
